\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Power counting at a Gaussian reference}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{For #{0<\lambda<1}, put #{L=\lambda^{-1/2}>1} and define the
positive number #{d_h} by #{p=L^{d_h}}. The eigenvalue of
\ref{fdip-01CY} is
##{p(p\lambda)^{-n/2}
=L^{d_h-n(d_h-2)/2}.}
Thus the site sum of the #{n}-th Wick power is relevant, marginal
or irrelevant according to whether this number is greater than,
equal to or less than one. The degree-two eigenvalue is
#{\lambda^{-1}}, agreeing with the uniform precision direction.
The quartic is marginal exactly when #{p\lambda^2=1}.
The degree-zero observable changes only the normalizing constant of
an interaction probability.}

\p{This is the scalar canonical-dimension comparison of
\citet{Appendix, (A.14), p. 189}{wilson1974renormalization}:
the field dimension is #{(d_h-2)/2} and a degree-#{n} site sum
carries exponent #{d_h-n(d_h-2)/2}. In that comparison #{d_h}
takes the place of their spatial dimension. Here it is read from
#{p} and #{\lambda}; the hierarchical field has not been identified
with a field on a Euclidean lattice. The continuum comparison is
cited, not proved here.}

\p{The finite conventions also agree with
\citet{arXiv v2, Definition 4.1.7, p. 54}{bauerschmidt2019renormalisation}
when #{p=L^d} and #{\lambda=L^{-2}}: their averaging projector
#{Q_j} is #{E_j}, their #{P_j} is #{P_{j-1}}, and their nonnegative
operator #{-\Delta_{H,k}} is #{H_k}. Their Gaussian convolution
and Wick ordering in
\citet{arXiv v2, Proposition 2.1.6 and Definition 2.1.8, pp. 31-32}{bauerschmidt2019renormalisation}
act by finite polynomials in differentiation on a polynomial
observable. The identities in \ref{fdip-01CX} and \ref{fdip-01CY}
prove the corresponding formulas with the present class means and
counting pairings.}

\p{The interaction step in \ref{fdip-01CY} is linear conditional
expectation. For a bounded-below polynomial interaction, it is the
first right derivative at zero coupling of the exact fiber
interaction, by \ref{fdip-00GN}. A finite interaction probability
additionally requires integrability of its weight. The exact
finite-coupling map is not asserted to preserve a Wick degree.}
